\subsection{Tor Bridge Verification via Manipulated Windows Event ID 4674 Logs}

To covertly verify Tor bridge availability and performance metrics, teams manipulate Windows Security Event ID 4674 logs (object access with special privileges) by embedding encrypted performance data in custom fields.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Technique:} Inject encrypted JSON metrics into event log \texttt{ObjectName} or \texttt{ProcessName} fields.
    \item \textbf{Verification:} Counterpart parses events with predefined decryption keys.
\end{itemize}

\noindent\textbf{Example PowerShell snippet to write manipulated Event ID 4674:}
\begin{verbatim}
$encryptedMetrics = "U2FsdGVkX1+xyzEncryptedPayload==" # AES-encrypted JSON performance data

$eventMessage = @"
ObjectName: $encryptedMetrics
ProcessName: C:\Windows\System32\tor.exe
AccessMask: 0x1F0FFF
"@

# Use New-WinEvent or native API to write Security log event with ID 4674
Write-EventLog -LogName Security -Source "Microsoft Windows security auditing." -EventId 4674 -EntryType Information -Message $eventMessage
\end{verbatim}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Parsing and verification} requires access to the security event logs and decryption with agreed symmetric keys.

---

This multi-stage protocol leverages cryptographic primitives, blockchain timelocks, covert linguistic channels, and system log manipulation to ensure robust, deniable coordination of distributed penetration testing teams operating in hostile or surveilled environments.

